---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/SiteFooter.astro';
---
<BaseLayout>
  <SiteHeader />

  <main id="top">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-content">
        <div class="hero-text-block">
          <p class="eyebrow">Corporate Finance • Financial Planning • Capital Allocation • Independent Research</p>

          <h1>Sergey Kyunttsel</h1>

          <p class="subtitle">
            Corporate-finance and capital-allocation professional, business owner, and independent
            researcher working across FP&amp;A, investment analysis, financial planning, lifecycle economics,
            and realized value.
          </p>

          <p class="hero-text">
            Sergey Kyunttsel, PhD in Economics, brings more than 15 years of corporate-finance,
            FP&amp;A, capital-allocation, business-owner, and investment-analysis experience. His
            professional and research work connects financial modeling, executive decision support,
            lifecycle economic analysis, project implementation, and long-term value realization.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="hero-photo-wrap">
          <img class="hero-photo" src="/assets/sergey-photo.png" alt="Sergey Kyunttsel, PhD in Economics" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="about" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">About</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Finance and economic analysis for decisions that must work in practice.</h2>

        <p>
          Sergey's professional work spans corporate finance, FP&amp;A, financing, financial modeling,
          investment evaluation, business ownership, and executive decision support. His current U.S.
          professional focus includes financial planning and private wealth, alongside independent
          research into how capital decisions produce durable, measurable results.
        </p>

        <p>
          Current research areas include energy-efficiency project selection, projected versus
          achieved savings, lifecycle capital-allocation losses, industrial lighting modernization,
          distributed generation and zero-overflow industrial supply arrangements, and the conversion
          of technical improvements into verified economic value.
        </p>

        <p>
          Professional affiliations include Senior Member, Association of Energy Engineers; Member,
          Cogeneration &amp; Distributed Generation Institute; Member, IEEE; and Member, IEEE Power
          &amp; Energy Society.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="credentials" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Credentials &amp; Professional Development</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Education, examinations, and continuing development.</h2>

        <p>
          Sergey holds a PhD in Economics. He passed the Securities Industry Essentials examination
          in June 2026, the Series 65 examination in July 2026, and the Series 63 examination in August
          2026. He has been approved for CFP Board's Accelerated Path, completed the 2026 Financial
          Planning Externship, and is proceeding to the CFP Capstone with the CFP exam planned for
          November 2026. He is also preparing for the Series 7 examination, which requires firm
          sponsorship, and is registered for the CFA Level I exam in February 2027.
        </p>

        <p>
          These examination and professional-development milestones are not presented as an active
          securities license, registration, or offer of financial-advisory services.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="research" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Research &amp; Publications</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Research, publications, and professional writing.</h2>

        <p>
          Sergey's research and professional writing focus on lifecycle economic outcomes,
          capital-allocation quality, energy-resource efficiency, industrial modernization,
          and the gap between projected and achieved economic effect.
        </p>

        <p>
          His work connects applied project experience with broader questions of economic
          decision-making: how organizations compare alternatives, allocate capital, manage
          implementation risk, verify achieved results, and evaluate whether modernization
          projects produce durable value in practice.
        </p>

        <p>
          Selected scholarly manuscripts in this research area are currently under editorial review.
        </p>

        <p>
          Three practitioner articles have been accepted and are in production for Association of
          Energy Engineers publications.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Published by CFO.com on September 10, 2026:</strong>
          <a href="https://www.cfo.com/news/your-capital-reapprovals-ask-the-wrong-question/829818/"
             target="_blank"
             rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Your Capital Reapprovals Ask the Wrong Question</em></a>.
          The article argues that capital reapproval should test whether an approved project still
          ranks first among alternatives competing for the same capital, not merely whether the project
          continues to clear its original hurdle. It proposes a bounded reranking review built around
          decisive assumptions, policy thresholds, and a named finance owner.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Published by IFMA's FMJ Magazine on August 10, 2026:</strong>
          <a href="https://fmj.ifma.org/the-warranty-is-still-valid-26"
             target="_blank"
             rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>The Warranty is Still Valid: Why is the organization still paying?</em></a>.
          The article examines why formal warranty coverage may still leave facility owners with
          significant lifecycle operating burdens, and proposes linking contract records with operating
          records to make those costs, responsibilities, and recurring failures visible.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Published by CFO.com on July 21, 2026:</strong>
          <a href="https://www.cfo.com/news/why-modernization-projects-pass-the-payback-test-and-still-lose-value/825657/"
             target="_blank"
             rel="noopener noreferrer"><em>Why Modernization Projects Pass the Payback Test and Still Lose Value</em></a>.
          The article explains why modernization projects can satisfy initial payback requirements yet
          lose value during implementation, and argues that finance ownership should extend beyond
          capital approval to preserve traceability between the approved business case, delivered scope,
          and realized results.
        </p>

        <p>The SELRA research-agenda working paper is publicly available on SSRN.</p>

        <div class="link-grid">
          <a href="https://www.cfo.com/news/your-capital-reapprovals-ask-the-wrong-question/829818/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFO.com — Capital reapprovals</a>
          <a href="https://fmj.ifma.org/the-warranty-is-still-valid-26" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FMJ article</a>
          <a href="https://www.cfo.com/news/why-modernization-projects-pass-the-payback-test-and-still-lose-value/825657/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CFO.com — Modernization</a>
          <a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=6974138" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSRN</a>
          <a href="https://orcid.org/0009-0005-0190-5826" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ORCID</a>
          <a href="https://scholar.google.com/citations?user=2Abz9i0AAAAJ" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Scholar</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="selra" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">SELRA</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Selection and Economic-Lifecycle Realization Architecture.</h2>

        <p>
          SELRA, the Selection and Economic-Lifecycle Realization Architecture,
          is Sergey's research program for evaluating industrial energy-resource
          investment decisions across project selection, lifecycle economic
          evaluation, realization, and verification.
        </p>

        <p>
          SELRA.org is the dedicated research-program site. This site remains
          Sergey's broader personal and professional identity site, connecting
          the research program with economics, capital allocation, industrial
          energy systems, and Sergey's wider professional work.
        </p>

        <p>The SELRA research-agenda working paper is publicly available on SSRN.</p>

        <div class="link-grid">
          <a href="https://selra.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SELRA.org</a>
          <a href="https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=6974138" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            SELRA: A Research Agenda for Reducing Lifecycle Capital-Allocation Losses in Energy-Resource Efficiency Projects
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="technical" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Technical Background</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Public technical contribution and developer background.</h2>

        <p>
          Sergey's earlier public technical work includes Java SE/EE, JSF,
          PrimeFaces, Spring, Hibernate, and web application development, with
          long-standing participation on Stack Overflow under the historical handle
          <strong>skuntsel</strong>.
        </p>

        <p>
          This technical background supports his broader work in applied research,
          data analysis, lifecycle modeling, economic modeling, and decision-support
          tools.
        </p>

        <div class="link-grid">
          <a href="https://github.com/skuntsel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>
          <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/1820286/sergey-kyunttsel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Stack Overflow</a>
        </div>

        <div class="stack-flair">
          <a href="https://stackexchange.com/users/2038555/sergey-kyunttsel"
             target="_blank"
             rel="noopener noreferrer"
             aria-label="Sergey Kyunttsel on Stack Exchange">
            <img
              src="https://stackexchange.com/users/flair/2038555.png"
              width="208"
              height="58"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
              alt="Sergey Kyunttsel’s Stack Exchange profile flair"
              title="Sergey Kyunttsel on Stack Exchange" />
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="energy" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Energy &amp; Modernization</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Energy-resource efficiency and industrial modernization.</h2>

        <p>
          Sergey's applied work and research interests include industrial energy projects where
          engineering conditions, economic modeling, implementation discipline, and post-project
          verification jointly determine the final result.
        </p>

        <p>
          Relevant areas include industrial lighting modernization, distributed generation,
          zero-overflow industrial supply arrangements, HVAC and other industrial energy systems,
          and project-realization conditions that affect whether projected savings become achieved
          savings.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="capital" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Capital Allocation</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Lifecycle economics and capital-allocation quality.</h2>

        <p>
          Modernization and efficiency projects are often presented as cost-saving initiatives.
          In practice, they are capital-allocation decisions. A technically reasonable project may
          still underperform if alternatives are compared too narrowly, implementation risks are ignored,
          or achieved results are not measured against the original economic logic.
        </p>

        <p>
          Sergey's current writing explores energy-efficiency project selection, lifecycle
          capital-allocation losses, realized-value analysis, and the evidence and verification
          practices needed to improve the conversion of committed capital into measurable
          long-term value.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="financial" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Financial Strategy</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Corporate finance, financial planning, and private wealth.</h2>

        <p>
          Sergey's experience includes corporate finance, FP&amp;A, capital allocation, financial
          modeling, financing, investment analysis, business ownership, and executive decision
          support. His current U.S. professional focus includes financial planning and private wealth,
          particularly where corporate-finance judgment, investment analysis, business-owner planning,
          and long-term client relationships intersect.
        </p>

        <p>
          This perspective complements his research and energy-efficiency work: better outcomes
          rarely come from isolated decisions. They come from structure, disciplined analysis,
          and choices that remain aligned through implementation. This site does not present
          investment-management or financial-advisory services.
        </p>

        <p>Professional profile:</p>

        <div class="link-grid">
          <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/sergeykyunttsel" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LinkedIn</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="contact" class="section">
      <div class="section-label">Contact</div>

      <div class="section-body">
        <h2>Contact</h2>

        <p>
          For research, publications, expert review, jury or panel invitations,
          collaboration, methodology application or use, media, and general
          professional inquiries:
        </p>

        <p class="contact-line">
          <a href="mailto:contact@sergeykyunttsel.com">contact@sergeykyunttsel.com</a>
        </p>

        <div class="contact-actions">
          <a
            class="contact-cta"
            href="https://calendly.com/sergey-kyunttsel/research-review-methodology"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Schedule Inquiry
          </a>
        </div>

        <p class="contact-note">
          Please do not submit confidential financial, legal, tax, medical,
          account, or other personal information by email or through any general
          inquiry channel.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
